.TH "EXTRACT.SH" "1" "November 2026" "arran4/dotfiles" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
extract.sh \- extract one or more archive files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBextract.sh ARCHIVE...\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Extract archives by filename suffix, using tar, unzip, unrar\-compatible rar, 7z and standard decompression utilities as available. Supports .tar.bz2, .tar.gz, .tar.xz, .tbz2, .tgz, .tar, .bz2, .gz, .zip, .rar, .7z and .Z. Files are extracted into the current working directory. Invalid files and unsupported formats produce diagnostic text.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
extract.sh download.tar.xz
extract.sh one.zip two.tar.gz
.fi
.SH NOTES
Review archive contents and trustworthiness before extracting them into a directory with important files.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Requires the extractor appropriate to the suffix: tar for tar
archives, unzip for zip, rar for rar, 7z for 7z, and bunzip2,
gunzip or uncompress for standalone compressed inputs. It chooses
the command by filename extension rather than file contents.
.SH PROCESSING AND LIMITATIONS
Files are processed in argument order. Archive members are extracted
relative to the current working directory, not beside the archive.
Standalone .gz and .bz2 inputs pass through gunzip or bunzip2, which
normally replace the compressed input on successful decompression.
.PP
An unknown suffix or nonexistent input prints a diagnostic without
necessarily making the command fail. A failed actual extractor
stops processing because the script enables set -e.
.SH SAFE EXTRACTION
.nf
mkdir -p /tmp/archive-review
cd /tmp/archive-review
extract.sh ~/Downloads/example.tar.gz
.fi
.PP
Inspect archive members before extraction. Untrusted archives may
include parent-directory traversal, absolute paths or symlinks, and
multiple archives may overwrite each other's output files.
.SH SEE ALSO
compress.sh(1)
